ary projective three-weight codes and generalizes the binary conjecture. Its general validity is left open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nMichael Kiermaier, Sascha Kurz, Patrick Solé, Michael Stoll and Alfred Wassermann, “On strongly walk regular graphs, triple sum sets and their codes”, arXiv:2012.06160 (2022).\n\ncoding-theoryfinite-fieldsthree-weight-codes\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn strongly walk regular graphs, triple sum sets and their... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/on-strongly-walk-regular-graphs-triple-sum-sets-and-their-codes-9058-1963804)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This paper classifies feasible parameters for strongly walk regular graphs (SWRGs) derived from projective three-weight codes, focusing on binary and ternary cases. ... We provide classifications of the feasible parameters of these codes in the binary and ternary case for medium size code lengths. ... This equation defines a plane algebraic curve; we use methods from algorithmic arithmetic geometry to show that for $s = 5$ and $s = 7$, there are only the obvious solutions, and we conjecture this to remain true for all (odd) $s \\ge 9$. ... @article{wassermann2022on, title = {On strongly walk regular graphs, triple sum sets and their codes}, author = {Wassermann, Alfred and Kiermaier, Michael and Stoll, Michael and others}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2012.06160}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.CO}, }\n\nBack ArXiv math.CO On strongly walk regular graphs, triple sum sets and their codes\n\nArXiv math.CO\n\n# On strongly w