Stoll, Wassermann, “On strongly walk regular graphs, triple sum sets and their codes,” Designs, Codes and Cryptography 91 (2023), arXiv:2012.06160.\n## Novelty assessment\n\nTYPE1\n\nClassification rationale: The counterexample is valid but very small and elementary: a projective ternary $[7,3]_3$[7,3]3​ code from the union of two lines in $\\mathrm{PG}(2,3)$PG(2,3), with weights $3,5,6$3,5,6. It disproves the conjecture as stated only because the conjecture omitted an integrality/divisibility condition. This is mathematically useful as a correction/erratum, but not substantial enough for a standalone combinatorics paper.\n\nLiterature check: I found no evidence that this exact counterexample or an explicit disproof of Conjecture 3.4 has appeared after the Kiermaier–Kurz–Solé–Stoll–Wassermann paper. Searches targeted the conjecture wording, the weight identity $w_1+w_2+w_3=3(1-1/q)n$w 1​+w 2​+w 3​=3(1−1/q)n, the paper title, “projective three-weight code”, “strongly walk regular graphs”, and the specific ternary $[7,3]$[7,3] weight set $3,5,6$3,5,6, across arXiv/DOI metadata, citation-style sources, web search endpoints, GitHub/issues, and related open web sources. No prior resolution surfaced. The underlying code geometry is standard, but I did not find it cited as a counterexample to this conjecture.\n\nCitation: Kiermaier, Kurz, Solé, Stoll, Wassermann, “On strongly walk regular graphs, triple sum sets and their codes,” Designs, Codes and Cryptography 91 (2023), arXiv:2012.06160.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraphical Enumeration and Stained Glass Windows, 1:... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/graphical-enumeration-and-stained-glass-windows-1-rectangular-grids-4480-2008415)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Back ArXiv math.CO Graphical Enumeration and Stained Glass Windows, 1: Rectangular Grids ... Paper ID: 2009.07918\nA few of these problems have been solved, a classical example being the the graph K_n formed when all pairs of vertices of a regular n-gon are joined by chords, which was analyzed by Poonen and Rubinstein in 1998. Most of these problems are unsolved, however, and this two-part article provides data from a number of such problems as well as colored illustrations, which are often reminiscent of stained glass windows. The polygons considered include rectangles, hollow rectangles (or frames), triangles, pentagons, pentagrams, crosses, etc., as well as figures formed by drawing semicircles joining equally-spaced points on a line. %The paper ends with a brief discussion of the problem of how to %design aesthetically pleasing colorings for these graphs. This first part discusses rectangular grids. The 1 X n grids, or equally the graphs K_{n+1,n+1}, were studied by Legendre and Griffiths, and here we investigate the number of cells with a given number of edges and the number of nodes with a given degree. We have only partial results for the m X n rectangles, including upper bounds on the numbers of nodes and cells.\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2009.07918\n\nLicense: http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\n\nComments: 25 pages, 26 references, 20 figures, 12 tables. Added a table and a conjecture\n\nCategories:\n\nmath.CO\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### How to Cite This Paper\n\n[Button: BibTeX] [Button: APA] [Button: MLA] [Button: Chicago] [Button: IEEE]\n\n@article{blomberg2020graphical, title = {Graphical Enumeration and Stained Glass Windows, 1: Rectangular Grids}, author = {Blomberg, Lars and Sloane, N. J. A. and Shannon, Scott R.}, year = {2020}, eprint = {2009.07918}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.CO}, }\n\n### Authors\n\nLB Lars Blomberg NJ N. J. A. Sloane 383 papers SR Scott R. Shannon--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCyclically covering subspaces in $\\mathbb{F}_2^n | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/332011251_Cyclically_covering_subspaces_in_mathbbF_2n)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1903.10613 ... We show that $h_2(p)= 2$ for every prime p such that 2 is a primitive root modulo p, which, assuming Artin's conjecture, answers a question of Peter Cameron from 1991.\n\n# Cyclically covering subspaces in $\\mathbb{F}_2^n\n\n  * March 2019\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1903.10613\n\nAuthors:\n\nJames Aaronson\n\nJames Aaronson\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nCarla Groenland\n\nCarla Groenland\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nTom Johnston\n\n  * University of Edinburgh\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nA subspace of $\\mathbb{F}_2^n$ is called cyclically covering if every vector in $\\mathbb{F}_2^n$ has a cyclic shift which is inside the subspace. Let $h_2(n)$ denote the largest possible codimension of a cyclically covering subspace of $\\mathbb{F}_2^n$. We show that $h_2(p)= 2$ for every prime p such that 2 is a primitive root modulo p, which, assuming Artin's conjecture, answers a question of Peter Cameron from 1991. We also prove various bounds on $h_2(ab)$ depending on $h_2(a)$ and $h_2(b)$ and extend some of our results to a more general set-up proposed by Cameron, Ellis and Raynaud.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest file PDF\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\np), this answers the original question of the first author in the negative. We remark that Artin’s conjecture is widely believed; indeed, as shown by Hooley [6], it is implied by the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis. However, the first author’s question remains open in the strict (i.e., unconditional) sense. In [1], Aaronson, Groenland and Johnston prove several other elegant results on the problems we consider above; for example, they prove that if q is an odd prime, and p > q is a prime such that q is a primitive root modulo p, then h_q(p) = 0. It is known, by a result of Heath-Brown [5], that for all but at most two primes q, there are infinitely many primes p for which q is a primitive root modulo p, so the aforementioned result of Aaronson, Groenland and Johnson implies (unconditionally) that for all but at most two odd primes q, h_q(n) does not tend to infinity as n tends to infinity over integers coprime to q. (https://qmro.qmul.ac.uk/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/58045/Ellis%20Smallest%20cyclically%20covering%202019%20Accepted.pdf?isAllowed=y&sequence=2)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; Johnston, Cyclically covering subspaces in F₂ⁿ, preprint, March 2019. arXiv:1903.10613. ... Cameron (Ed.), Research Problems, in: Proceedings of the 13th British Combinatorial Conference, *Discrete Math. ... Heath-Brown, Artin’s conjecture for primitive roots, *The Quarterly Journal of Mathematics* **37** (1986), 27–38. ... Martin Isaacs, *Finite Group Theory*, Graduate Studies in Mathematics vol.\nWe would also like to thank two anonymous referees for their careful reading of the paper, and for their helpful suggestions.\n\n**References**\n\n[1] J. Aaronson, C. Groenland and T. Johnston, Cyclically covering subspaces in F₂ⁿ, preprint, March 2019. arXiv:1903.10613.\n\n[2] P. J. Cameron (Ed.), Research Problems, in: Proceedings of the 13th British Combinatorial Conference, *Discrete Math.* **125** (1994), 407–417.\n\n[3] P. J. Cameron, P. Frankl and W. M. Kantor, Intersecting families of finite sets and fixed-point-free 2-elements, *Europ. J. Combin.* **10** (1989), 149–160.\n\n[4] B. Fein, W. M. Kantor and M. Schacher, Relative Brauer groups II, *J. Reine Angew. Math.* **328** (1981), 39–57.\n\n[5] D.R. Heath-Brown, Artin’s conjecture for primitive roots, *The Quarterly Journal of Mathematics* **37** (1986), 27–38.\n\n[6] C. Hooley, On Artin’